 "content": {
  "subject": "Electrical Engineering",
  "topic": "Extension of Range of PMMC Instruments",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "hook": "Can a one milliamp meter survive a ten ampere blast?",
  "question": "A PMMC ammeter has an internal resistance of 99 ohms and gives full scale deflection for 1 milliampere. What shunt resistance is required to extend its range to 100 milliamperes?",
  "options": [
   "1 ohm",
   "0.99 ohms",
   "1.01 ohms",
   "9.9 ohms"
  ],
  "correctIndex": 0,
  "answerLine": "The required shunt resistance is exactly 1 ohm.",
  "explanation": [
   "To expand the range, we divert extra current around the delicate meter movement using a parallel shunt resistor.",
   "First calculate the multiplying factor m, defined as the total target current divided by meter deflection current.",
   "Dividing 100 milliamperes by 1 milliampere gives an operating multiplying factor m equal to 100.",
   "The standard shunt equation relates meter resistance Rm and shunt resistance Rsh by Rm divided by m minus 1."
  ],
  "funFact": "Shunts are typically made of manganin because its resistance barely changes with temperature variations!",
  "outro": "Master your electrical instrument formulas and follow along for more exam practice questions!",
